=== Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "A", dense: true)[
  Muscle lacks the enzymes for the yeast pathway, so it forms lactic acid.
]

#sol-item(2, "C", dense: true)[
  The alveoli provide an enormous, thin, moist surface for diffusion.
]

#sol-item(3, "C", dense: true)[
  Contraction flattens the diaphragm, increasing the volume of the thorax and lowering the pressure.
]

#sol-item(4, "D", dense: true)[
  Respiration is continuous; in daylight photosynthesis simply outpaces it.
]

#sol-item(5, "C", dense: true)[
  Glucose and oxygen are the raw materials; carbon dioxide, water and energy are the products.
]

#sol-item(6, "B", dense: true)[
  Yeast converts glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide — the basis of brewing and baking.
]

#sol-item(7, "B", dense: true)[
  Light absorption is done by chlorophyll, not by respiration.
]

#sol-item(8, "B", dense: true)[
  A thin barrier allows rapid diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide.
]

#sol-item(9, "A", dense: true)[
  Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky (cloudy).
]

#sol-item(10, "D", dense: true)[
  Flowing blood removes oxygen and brings carbon dioxide continuously.
]

#sol-item(11, "A", dense: true)[
  The products still contain chemical energy, so less of the glucose's energy is released.
]

#sol-item(12, "B", dense: true)[
  After vigorous exercise, extra oxygen is needed to oxidise lactic acid, largely in the liver.
]

#sol-item(13, "A", dense: true)[
  Respiration uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water vapour, and the air is also warmed.
]

#sol-item(14, "A", dense: true)[
  Incomplete rings of cartilage keep the airway open while still allowing the oesophagus to expand.
]

#sol-item(15, "B", dense: true)[
  Breathing is the mechanical movement of air; the others are respiration or diffusion.
]

#sol-item(16, "D", dense: true)[
  Energy is transferred to ATP, the cell's usable energy currency.
]

#sol-item(17, "B", dense: true)[
  Relaxation of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles reduces thoracic volume and raises the pressure.
]

#sol-item(18, "C", dense: true)[
  The lungs have no muscle; they are inflated by the pressure difference created around them.
]

#sol-item(19, "D", dense: true)[
  Exercise raises carbon dioxide in the blood, stimulating faster and deeper breathing.
]

#sol-item(20, "A", dense: true)[
  Gills provide a large, thin, well-supplied surface in water.
]

#sol-item(21, "D", dense: true)[
  Gases must dissolve before diffusing, so the surface must be moist, not dry.
]

#sol-item(22, "A", dense: true)[
  Lactic acid lowers the pH of the muscle, impairing enzyme action.
]

#sol-item(23, "D", dense: true)[
  That is the aerobic equation; the reverse is photosynthesis.
]

#sol-item(24, "A", dense: true)[
  The lungs excrete carbon dioxide and water vapour in exhaled air.
]

#sol-item(25, "C", dense: true)[
  Tar paralyses the cilia and destroys alveolar walls, reducing gas exchange and trapping mucus.
]

#sol-item(26, "B", dense: true)[
  With no light there is no photosynthesis, so the continuous respiration releases carbon dioxide.
]

#sol-item(27, "C", dense: true)[
  Air reaches the tissues directly through the tracheae and tracheoles.
]

#sol-item(28, "D", dense: true)[
  Chlorophyll is made by the plant for photosynthesis and is not a product of respiration.
]

#sol-item(29, "C", dense: true)[
  Carbon dioxide is the main stimulus to the respiratory centre in the brain.
]

#sol-item(30, "B", dense: true)[
  The extra breathing repays the oxygen debt by oxidising accumulated lactic acid.
]

